  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    A refugee encampment carved into the Nightshelf's eastern ice-face during the tail-end of the Orca Incursion, built under the direction of Qingoqa aihua'Mist-wrought. The caverns were never intended for permanent habitation at this scale; the Nightshelf widened them under duress, and the work shows — uneven walls bearing visible tool-marks, support pillars that lean wrong, unmortared ice-blocks stacked by penguins who did not have time to do it properly. The place has no official designation. Locals call it 'the Scar' or 'Qingoqa's Folly,' depending on temperament.
    
    It persists now as a permanent settlement, though *permanent* may overstate the intentionality. Aurora Stack survivors — penguins who lost spires, who watched crystal-gardens burn — claimed alcoves during the crisis and simply never left. Names are carved into the ice with bone-picks, fire-hardened sticks, and in some cases bare claws. The Nightshelf garrison shares fire-core heat during winter months and does not press questions about origins or intentions. The grim courtesy of those who remember their own displacement requires no policy to sustain it.
    
    ## The Memorial Wall and the Shadow-Ledgers
    
    Three winters ago, an unnamed carver began a memorial wall listing the dead of both colonies. It has become the only site where Aurora Stack and Nightshelf penguins stand together without friction — a fact worth noting plainly, since such sites are not common.
    
    The wall's significance extends beyond commemoration. It preserves founding-era names that the Promenade Ward's own ice-memory was altered to forget. The families Moqai and Xuelin — who froze during the founding's third winter after Summit-Lord Yahailong diverted fire-cores to western perimeter defenses — were erased from Aurora records through Snowdrift-numb manipulation taught by Hainou mewei'Frostglass. Their names survive here because they were carved by different hands into a different archive.
    
    The Scathe also maintains shadow-ledgers: courier logs, depot manifests, and requisition records kept in ink on hide rather than frozen into ice-memory. The Nightshelf's longstanding distrust of Aurora's archive medium has, in this case, produced the only founding-era records that Snowdrift-numb manipulation cannot reach. It was from these ledgers that Zhinghoua lang'Gleam, an Aurora Stack investigator, later recovered the fire-core shipment discrepancies that exposed Yahailong's actions.
    
    The deep ice here carries its own testimony — thermal distress, acoustic impressions workers describe as *a weight that isn't cold*. I have not visited the Scathe personally. The ice-memory readings from adjacent strata are consistent with sustained trauma events. They require no embellishment.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771589854839_0", anchor: "during the tail-end of the Orca Incursion", text: "The cataloguing index dates the Scathe's founding to the Frozen Peace. This text — my text — says Orca Incursion. Both cannot be right. The Frozen Peace date reflects formalization: when the Nightshelf garrison assigned a permanent detail and the alcoves received official tolerance. Qingoqa broke ground while raids were still active. Penguins carved those first shelters under threat of attack, not in its aftermath. The distinction matters to anyone who slept there.", type: :pedantic, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771589854839_1", anchor: "The Nightshelf's longstanding distrust of Aurora's archive medium", text: "An ice-archivist endorsing ink-on-hide as the superior record. Every founding-era entry in my own archive is vulnerable to the same manipulation that erased the Moqai and Xuelin names. The shadow-ledgers are not. I am aware of the irony.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771589854839_2", anchor: "fire-core shipment discrepancies that exposed Yahailong's actions", text: "Zhinghoua lang'Gleam compared the ledger figures against the public Flipper Accord's allocation schedules, and the discrepancies were damning. They would have been worse had she used the second sealed draft, which sets allocation ratios nearly fifteen percent higher than the published version. Yahailong diverted fire-cores from a supply already less than what the Nightshelf had originally agreed to provide. The scandal has a floor beneath the floor. I found those drafts. I did not share them with Zhinghoua. I did not know her investigation existed until the results were announced. Whether earlier access would have changed the outcome — I have stopped asking myself that question, which is not the same as answering it.", type: :correction, display: :full
